  <p>Hipo was an application for managing an iPod from the GNOME desktop. This first article does not try to close the story: it opens the archive and proposes a way to reconstruct it with code, context and memory.</p>

  <h2>The context</h2>
  <p>At the time, writing a desktop application in C# and Mono meant working close to a technological frontier that was still taking shape. The project connected a concrete need with a community, a toolkit and a free software ecosystem.</p>

  <blockquote>Hipo is a visual entry point into a much broader journey: it was not everything I did, but it gives us somewhere concrete to begin.</blockquote>

  <h2>What we will reconstruct</h2>
  <p>The series will follow the project’s path: how the idea appeared, how the code was organized, what role GNOME and Debian played, and what lessons remained after the project.</p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>Older projects do not only tell us which technologies we used. They also show how we learned, collaborated and did the invisible work that allowed an application to reach other people.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
